# Every file here is CONTENT-ADDRESSED: its name is the hash of its bytes, and the player refuses any
# file whose bytes do not hash to the name it asked for. That check is the whole reason a blob can be
# cached forever without revalidation - so anything that rewrites bytes in transit breaks the store.
#
# Git's end-of-line conversion is exactly such a rewrite. It turned a 2329-byte timeline.json with CRLF
# into 2240 bytes with LF, and the player correctly refused it: "checksum mismatch for
# blobs/f8f4cce3ae608dd2.json: got f56186529e120134". The file was intact on disk and intact on the
# server; it was intact differently.
#
# So: NO conversion, for anything, ever. `-text` tells git to treat every path as binary - store the
# bytes it was given and hand back exactly those bytes.
* -text
